{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויצו את הראשון", :translit "vayetzav et harishon", :gloss "and he commanded the first"} {:he "את", :translit "et", :gloss "(untranslated object marker)"} {:he "ד\"פ את", :translit "d'f et", :gloss "four times et"} {:he "כנגד ד' מלכיות", :translit "keneged d' malkhuyot", :gloss "corresponding to four kingdoms"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["את"]}], :frame {:text "The four exiles suffered by Israel are traditionally identified with four successive world empires — Babylon, Persia, Greece, and Rome/Edom — often called the four kingdoms (Daniel 7:3–7, Daniel 2:37–40). Esau's descendant Rome is associated with the final exile (Genesis 36:1). Jacob's agency toward his brother Esau in Genesis 32:18 thus prefigures Israel's subjugation under these empires.", :sources ["Daniel 7:3–7" "Daniel 2:37–40" "Genesis 36:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ולמי", :translit "ulemi", :gloss "and to whom"} {:he "ג' במסורה", :translit "g' b'masorah", :gloss "three in the Masorah"} {:he "ולמי כל חמדת ישראל", :translit "ulemi kol chemdat yisrael", :gloss "and to whom all the desire of Israel"} {:he "ולמי אני עמל", :translit "ulemi ani amel", :gloss "and to whom am I laboring"} {:he "כל חמדת ישראל ועמלם נטלו בני עשו", :translit "kol chemdat yisrael va'amalam natelu bnei esav", :gloss "all the desire of Israel and their labor the sons of Esau took"} {:he "ולמי אלה לפניך", :translit "ulemi eileh lefanekha", :gloss "and to whom are these before you"} {:he "כך אמר יעקב", :translit "kakh amar ya'akov", :gloss "thus said Jacob"} {:he "למי אני עמל לתת כל חמדת ישראל למי אלה לפניך", :translit "lemi ani amel latet kol chemdat yisrael lemi eileh lefanekha", :gloss "to whom am I laboring to give all the desire of Israel to whom are these before you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ולמי"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'chemdat yisrael' ('the desire of Israel') in 1 Chronicles 29:28 is applied by Targum to the kingdom of Israel and its treasures. The descendants of Esau (Edom/Rome) are identified as despoiling Israel's heritage (Genesis 36:8, identifying Esau with Edom). Jacob's rhetorical question in Genesis 32:18, sending gifts to Esau, is read as a lament that his accumulated wealth and labor will ultimately fall into the hands of Esau's descendants.", :sources ["1 Chronicles 29:28" "Genesis 36:8"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["את"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "את", :refs ["Genesis 1:1" "Genesis 1:4" "Genesis 1:7" "Genesis 1:16" "Genesis 1:21" "Genesis 1:22" "Genesis 1:25" "Genesis 1:27"], :n 5697, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ולמי"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ולמי", :refs ["Genesis 32:18" "1 Samuel 9:20" "Ecclesiastes 4:8"], :n 3, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:09:49.187849914Z", :model "glm-5.2"}